  "thumbnailAlt": "Employee Satisfaction Survey form beside a photo of engaged office team in business discussion around a laptop, fostering collaboration",
  "content": {
    "intro": "An employee satisfaction survey is a form that asks staff to rate their manager, workload, pay, growth, culture and recognition, so HR and leaders can see what keeps people engaged and what pushes them to leave. This template groups the questions by driver and ends with an eNPS score and open comments, so results are easy to act on.",
    "audience": "HR teams, people managers, founders and operations leads at companies from 10 to a few thousand staff.",
    "useCases": [
      "An annual or twice-yearly staff satisfaction pulse",
      "Checking morale after a reorganization, merger or office move",
      "Comparing satisfaction between teams, sites or shifts",
      "Measuring the effect of a new benefit, hybrid policy or manager training"
    ],
    "tips": [
      "Leave 'require sign-in' off and ask only for department and tenure, so answers stay anonymous and staff answer honestly.",
      "Use per-question charts in response analytics to compare drivers side by side and pick the two lowest to work on first.",
      "Set an open and close time window in Form Settings so the survey runs for a fixed two weeks and everyone answers in the same period."
    ],
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "What should an employee satisfaction survey include?",
        "answer": "Ratings on the main satisfaction drivers (manager support, workload, pay and benefits, growth, culture, recognition, tools), an employee Net Promoter Score (eNPS), basic segments like department and tenure, and open questions on what to keep and what to change."
      },
      {
        "question": "Should employee satisfaction surveys be anonymous?",
        "answer": "Usually yes. Anonymous surveys get more honest answers. Avoid asking for names and keep segments broad enough that no small group can be identified."
      },
      {
        "question": "How often should you run an employee satisfaction survey?",
        "answer": "Many companies run a full survey once or twice a year and short pulse checks quarterly. What matters more than frequency is sharing the results and acting on them before the next round."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is eNPS?",
        "answer": "Employee Net Promoter Score asks how likely staff are to recommend the company as a place to work on a 0–10 scale. It is the percentage of 9–10 answers minus the percentage of 0–6 answers."
      }
    ]
  },
